2026-10-06T08:39Z Claude note The "created" time above was estimated, not read: it is later than the real creation (the PR merged before 08:39Z). Added the trust principle (no open door, traceability instead), the parallel and background-merge criteria, and where the local CI runs.
2026-10-06T08:46Z Claude note Reframed traceability. The author meant tracing agents that get around the CI with git options, not who ran it. Added criteria for the credentials, a journal of updates to main, and a sweep of main; made the design runtime-neutral (git and the forge, no agent-runtime hook as its basis).

--- body ---
## Context

Ticket 1041 gave the harness a local runner that reproduces the forge CI
(`scripts/local-ci.sh`, merged in PR 1218). It does not change what lets `main`
move: the ruleset `main-required-checks` still requires seven forge checks
(validate-tickets, skill-lint, agnostic-guard, status-verb-guard,
pipefail-guard, grep-e-guard, pytest-guard) and has no bypass actor, so when the
forge's runners stop, PRs wait, as on 2026-10-05.

The author's aim is a CI native to git, in line with the dyne project (merge
requests as files, `ci pass`/`verdict` entries pinned to a revision; ticket 0270
in git-erg, "Hard points: CI, who executes?"). This ticket is that step: the
gate itself becomes local, so the forge is a host and not an authority.

Decided on 2026-10-06, so not to be reopened here:
- A bypass actor on the ruleset was applied and then reverted by the author:
  waiting is preferred to bypassing.
- A `pre-push` hook calling the local runner (option A) is dropped: it adds a
  slower run than the forge's, records nothing and is skipped by `--no-verify`.
- Trust is a base principle of IDH, so the gate leaves no open door: no bypass
  actor, no unguarded path to `main`. What it adds is traceability, of two
  things: what was run (when, by whom, on which host and revision), and any
  attempt to get around the gate with git options (`--no-verify`,
  `core.hooksPath`, `update-ref`, `branch -f`, `push --force`, an edit of the
  ruleset). Neither may depend on one agent runtime: the basis is git and the
  forge, which hold for any agent runtime and for a human. A per-runtime adapter
  (for instance a pre-tool hook) may add early warnings but is never what the
  trace rests on. A record is a statement that can be traced, not proof.
- Where the local CI runs: padme when it is reachable (skill `merge`, section
  "Before merging"), else the current host, named in the output.

Draft design, reviewed once by an independent reviewer (gpt-6.1-sol, medium,
read-only): `docs/2026-10-05-merge-gate-spec.md`. Its vocabulary replaces "CI":
checks (mechanical), verdict (judgement), merge gate (both, on the same
revision).

## Prerequisite

The author wants the content of the CI reviewed first: does it duplicate
`make check`, and what does it miss? Facts to start from: `make check` is not the
CI (it lacks validate-tickets, status-verb-guard, pipefail-guard, grep-e-guard,
tab-ifs-guard; it adds check-skills-drift and check-adapter-hooks); three CI jobs
run but are not required (personal-data-guard, tab-ifs-guard,
cross-pr-ticket-collision), so a failure of one does not stop a merge. File that
review as its own ticket; this one is blocked on its outcome.

## Scope

- A record tying a checks result to the exact commit that will become `main`
  (build the candidate merge first: merge-time hook effects such as `erg close`
  must be inside what was checked).
- `/merge` refuses without that record on the candidate and an APPROVED verdict
  on the branch head; supersession rule: the latest entry for a subject decides.
- The ruleset's required checks are removed or reduced. This is a forge setting,
  permanent and visible: it is the author's decision, taken at that step.
- A guard on `main` that cannot be bypassed from a clone (a
  `reference-transaction` hook). Installation must be verified: this repository
  sets `core.hooksPath=hooks`, so a hook placed in `.git/hooks` never runs.
- One machine moves `main`; the other fetches.

## Open decisions

1. Where the record lives: a note on the candidate commit (outside the tree) or a
   line in the MR file under dyne (inside the tree, needs a pin that survives
   appending). The first review found a tree-hash pin unsound: it let a changed
   `merges/dyne` binary and merge-time hook effects through.
2. Whether this is built in the harness first or as dyne (git-erg tickets
   0272/0273 define the `%dyne` format and `dyne merge`); the harness script is
   then a stand-in.
3. Which checks the gate runs, given the prerequisite; and how the record names
   who ran it (a human, or an agent with its model and runtime where known)
   without claiming to authenticate it. The harness already captures attribution
   (`scripts/attribution_record.py`, tickets 1032 to 1038): reuse it rather than
   invent a format. How it would plug in has not been checked.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The content review of the CI is filed and done, or this ticket records why
      it is not needed.
- [ ] The open decisions above are settled and written down.
- [ ] Each record names the time, the actor, the host, the revision checked, the
      command and its scope, and the tool versions: enough to answer "what was
      run, when, by whom" after the fact.
- [ ] The credentials that agent sessions use cannot edit the ruleset (the
      author's setting on the forge). Today the session's forge login can, and
      did on 2026-10-06 at the author's request; whether any session needs those
      rights has not been checked.
- [ ] Every update of `main` is journaled by a git hook (`reference-transaction`,
      which `--no-verify` does not skip): time, OS user, host, the command lines
      of the process chain, the git identity. A client that overrides
      `core.hooksPath` defeats the hook, which is why the next criterion exists.
- [ ] A periodic sweep checks that each commit that entered `main` is the merge
      of a PR whose required checks were green (or, once built, of a gate
      record), and reports any that is not. It uses only git and the forge's API,
      so it also catches a bypass that evaded every hook.
- [ ] Nothing above requires a particular agent runtime. A per-runtime detector
      is optional, and its absence is not a gap in the trace.
- [ ] The local checks run their jobs in parallel, with a concurrency of
      `min(jobs, nproc / 2)` (this host reports 24 threads, 12 cores; the author
      has a 32-core upgrade on order, not delivered). Each job has its own clone.
      The negative controls are re-run under parallelism before it is called
      safe: one infrastructure failure was seen when two runners shared the host,
      cause not established (a clash of container names is a hypothesis).
- [ ] The merge is made in the background once the gate passes, as the forge's
      auto-merge does, so waiting costs no attention.
- [ ] `/merge` refuses a branch with no record on its candidate commit, and a
      positive control shows it accepts one.
- [ ] A hook refuses an update of `main` that is not a gated merge, verified from
      a second worktree, and its installation is verified under
      `core.hooksPath`.
- [ ] The ruleset change is made by the author, and the day the forge is down is
      rehearsed once (a PR merged with the forge's runners unavailable or
      unconsulted).
